WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.280
Okay, let's get to audience questions first
one.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.200
Hey, David, could you lay out a basic financial

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.280
plan for someone who works and is middle class
but just has no idea what to do?

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.120
I understand lots of people aren't in a position
to save any money, but it would be great to

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.960
hear you talk about what middle class people
should be doing.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.250
Yeah, I, I, I've hesitated to do this, number
one

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:26.759
because I don't want to be condescending to
anybody, uh, based on their financial situation.

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:29.320
And also because I don't want to be giving
financial advice.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.490
So let me say none of this, this is all hypothetical.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:38.052
It's not advice for anyone specific situation,
but some basic principles are, if you don't

00:00:38.052 --> 00:00:42.800
have $1,000 in an emergency fund, you need
$1,000 in an emergency fund and not having

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.500
it, I would consider an emergency.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.400
You need to significantly curtail all spending
until you have that thousand dollars.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.900
We did a story not long ago about how some
very high percentage of Americans would not

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:59.810
be able to afford an unexpected $400 expense
without like putting it on a credit card or

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:00.950
borrowing from friends.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:01.950
That's a real problem.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.869
So you need $1,000 because of the healthcare
system we have, you probably in addition to

00:01:05.869 --> 00:01:09.369
the thousand should also have whatever your
deductible is.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:15.010
If your deductible yearly is $2,000 on health
insurance, you probably want that in addition

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.270
to the thousand dollar emergency fund.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:18.939
That's just a reality.

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:25.390
And then you want to be working towards a
three to six month, uh, not now.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:29.280
It's no longer an emergency fund, but sort
of an income replacement fund.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:36.979
The ideal scenario would be to have six months
of, if you, if your income went to zero, you

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:41.670
could split pay for everything you're currently
paying for for six months.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.909
That would be a great place to be.

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:47.689
And whether that's practical for different
people really depends on their situation.

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:50.560
You want to avoid high interest credit card
debt.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.609
It you ideally would just be paying, I use
my credit card like a debit card, I use it

00:01:54.609 --> 00:01:57.630
for frequent flyer miles and I pay it off
every month.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:58.630
Not everybody can do that.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:01:59.630
I understand that.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:04.690
But you want to be avoiding any fees and interests
that are not necessarily because it's just

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:05.719
takes away it's money.

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:08.450
You're just giving away to financial institutions.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.870
And then you do want to think a little bit
about your sort of general asset allocation.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:18.280
And this includes thinking about retirement
plans, what, how much cash should you have

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:24.970
versus money in tax deferred investment accounts
versus in real estate for example.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.629
So again, everybody's situation is very different.

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:34.629
Just having cash, uh, you know, having a whole
bunch of cash and then not really contributing

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:38.320
to anything that is going to grow in terms
of a retirement account or an investment account

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:39.500
is not particularly wise.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:45.079
I'm being deliberately vague again because
I am not giving specific financial advice.

00:02:45.079 --> 00:02:51.900
Uh, but as an example, I used to invest in
mutual funds in real estate investment trusts,

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.890
rei teas, reets as they're called, and it's
like a proxy for investing in real estate.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:02.890
When I actually started, uh, getting involved
in, in real estate directly with properties

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:06.829
that I'm involved with, uh, I realized it's
too much real estate.

00:03:06.829 --> 00:03:12.890
I should stop investing in the rates and maybe
look at reallocating that to something else.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:20.280
My philosophy on investing is don't pick stocks,
look for the lowest cost, broadest mutual

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.260
funds, and figure out what is your balance
between stocks and bonds.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.310
There are lots of tools out there.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:33.690
One says you take a, the number one 20 and
subtract or no, you take the number 100 and

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:38.530
subtract your age and that's the percent you
want to be in stock.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:43.280
So if you're in, if you're 30 years old, you
want to be 70% in stocks, which actually seems

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.140
a little bit conservative maybe for my taste,
but there's all these tools that will help

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:48.680
you figure that out.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:54.569
Uh, when it comes to things like mortgages,
very often, uh, it is not logical to take

00:03:54.569 --> 00:04:01.109
a huge chunk of money and pay off the mortgage
if you actually figure out the numbers financially

00:04:01.109 --> 00:04:05.310
because there is a tax benefit to paying mortgage
interest.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:10.980
And if your mortgage is 4% and you can invest
in something that historically returned 7%

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.790
you're better off taking the money and putting
it into the investment instead of into the

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:15.790
mortgage.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.910
But everybody's situation is different.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:24.539
There's a sort of psychological value to paying
down or paying off a mortgage, which what

00:04:24.539 --> 00:04:29.389
might make it the right thing for some people,
even if they are effectively accepting a lower

00:04:29.389 --> 00:04:30.889
return as a result.

00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:35.930
So these, it's really hard to have a, a, a
sort of overarching financial plan.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:42.400
But my sort of tips in general are you need
$1,000 right away, plus your health insurance

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.840
exposure, exposure, the amount you're exposed
to health insurance spending in an emergency

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:53.229
fund, working towards six months of expenses
in case you are nothing for six months.

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:58.660
You want to ensure that you are not on the
Hedonic treadmill and that your expenses are

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:03.630
properly attuned to your income and you don't
want to start spending more every time you

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.310
make a little more.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:10.050
Don't get into the cycle of living beyond
your means in terms of using high interest

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.780
credit card debt for anything other than absolute
necessities.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.979
Again, if you use it like a debit card to
accrue points and you pay it off and fold,

00:05:15.979 --> 00:05:16.979
that's okay.

00:05:16.979 --> 00:05:20.699
Uh, and then you want to be contributing as
much as you can in the most advantaged way

00:05:20.699 --> 00:05:22.270
possible towards retirement.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:29.360
So if your employer offers a 50% match up
to 6% of your income, you, if you can afford

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.240
it, want to be contributing 6% of your income
to that retirement plan to get the 3% from

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.919
your employer because that's quote free money.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:38.979
Of course nothing is really free.

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:43.180
You can make the case that your salary is
lower because your employer is already accounting

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:50.509
for that in the offer to match your contributions
50 cents on the dollar up to 6% but still

00:05:50.509 --> 00:05:53.800
it's money that's available and you want to
be taking advantage of that.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:00.050
Um, and, and really a lot of this stuff comes
to do, comes back to the tax impact.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:05.210
So it is important to read up on or have an
accountant that can advise you.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:10.580
For example, about with the recent Trump tax
reform, now that there has been a limit placed

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:16.699
on the deductability of certain types of taxes,
is it as advantageous to have that mortgage

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:21.949
or would it be better to look at paying it
down and investing the money elsewhere, et

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:22.949
cetera.

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:26.069
This is all very vague stuff, but I hope I'm
at least telling you sort of like a basic

00:06:26.069 --> 00:06:27.810
framework that I apply.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:32.950
Uh, and again, when it comes to any kind of
investment, one of your top priorities should

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:40.240
be reduce the cost, vanguard and betterment
without giving any particular endorsements,

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:48.340
have fees that are so low, sometimes 0.0 3.05%
compared to what sometimes or one or 2% management

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.620
fees for similar asset allocation in other
institutions.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.020
So pay very, very close attention to that.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:56.020
Okay.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.020
That does it for that.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.020
A question.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.180
Let's move on and I'm glad to take more questions
about this.

